WEBVTT

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:01.839
The engineer opens the ontology agent

00:00:01.839 --> 00:00:03.519
and ensures they are in plan

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:05.460
mode. Like a draft mode.

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.920
They prompt the agent with specific

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:09.960
instructions. Add a sale entity using

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.800
the sales table in the sales

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.180
report semantic model located in this

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.640
specific workspace. Okay, very specific.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.260
They then ask the agent to

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.300
preview the proposed definition, show

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:21.620
exactly how it mapped the data

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.640
bindings and explain the source evidence

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.200
it used to make those decisions,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:26.900
all while keeping the rest of

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.760
the live ontology completely untouched.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:30.840
So Plan Mode operates as a

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.639
secure sandbox. Exactly.

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.060
It is a critical enterprise safety

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:37.040
feature. Plan Mode allows you to

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.620
discover, draft, validate, query, and

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:41.440
inspect massive structural changes

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.460
without actually altering the live

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.519
production ontology that the rest of

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:46.680
the company is currently using.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:47.820
You can test it out first.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.000
You can see the draft entity

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.040
appear on the visual canvas, check

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:52.360
how it mapped the primary and

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:54.180
foreign keys, and run validation checks.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:56.040
If you realize the AI agent

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.160
picked the wrong column for the

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.920
primary key or completely missed a

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.540
crucial relationship, you can ask for

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.940
revisions right there in the sandbox,

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.660
or manually adjust it yourself.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.160
And when you are finally satisfied

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:07.600
that the logic holds up.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.380
Then and only then do you

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:11.039
deliberately switch the interface to

00:01:11.039 --> 00:01:12.820
ACT mode to apply the reviewed

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:14.560
proposal, merging it into the live

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:15.080
model.
